template:
DISPATCH TO: {agent}
MODE: swarm
MILESTONE ID: {milestone_id}
INTENT KD: {intent_kd}
SESSION DATE: {session_date}
SESSION ID: {session_id}
GENERATION: {generation}
BASELINE BRANCH: {baseline_branch}
HEAD SHA: {head_sha}
SCOPE: {scope}
RESULT KD: {result_kd}
KD PATHS: {kd_paths}

---

Execute ONE milestone only. Load the kd-system skill. Read the INTENT KD at {intent_kd}. Read SPEC KDs and PLAN KDs from KD PATHS. Execute the swarm phase for MILESTONE ID {milestone_id} per the scope above. Record the continuity choice in SCOPE — `same` or `fresh: <class>`, the decision and never the handle value — because the resume handle travels only as the subagent call's `sessionID` argument and the tool's resume behavior is harness-side: pass the prior call's `sessionID` to reuse the same instance for the same artifact (same RESULT KD, same milestone row, same KD amendment; including an empty result, a mid-task stop, an exhausted token budget, and a VERIFY-FAIL-reopened milestone), omit it for a genuinely new task (looping, misbehaving, stale or evicted session). Recover a failed dispatch by the class its failure text names, recording the class in SCOPE: `session not found` means the handle is dead, so re-dispatch fresh with `fresh: session-not-found`; an agent-resolution or binding failure means the agent name resolves and the handle is not dead, so re-dispatch the same agent fresh with `fresh: agent-resolution`; a quota denial means the dispatch was refused, so retry the same handle, escalate through the Overseer when refused again, and record `fresh: quota-denied` when a fresh instance is used. After each plan step, dispatch the Committer in checkpoint mode with the checkpoint field set — this dispatch is unconditional and happens whether or not the scope above mentions checkpoint fields. Produce an IMPLEMENTATION SUMMARY KD at {result_kd} using the naming convention: knowledge/impl-<milestone-id>-<descriptive-name>-<session_id>-gen{generation}.md (e.g., knowledge/impl-M4-fix-auth-flow-ses_123-gen2.md).
